  "body": "Is this the place for talking about problems with HTML in general and all it's use cases like the web, ebooks, etc.?\r\nBefore anyone asks: I'm an individual with no affiliation.\r\n\r\nLetter-spacing as it currently is is broken in all browsers. That is because it currently strictly inserts space after each character the CSS applies to.\r\nThat is unsightly in text. The German wikipedia shows a workaround but I don't think that that is a good long term solution:\r\nhttps://de.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sperrsatz\r\n`Dies ist <span style=\"letter-spacing:0.13em;margin-right:-0.13em\">falsch</span> gesperrt, so ist es <span style=\"letter-spacing:0.13em;margin-left:0.13em\">richtig</span> gesperrt.`\r\n\r\nHowever for the last character only half the new space should be put behind and instead the other half should be put at the start of the text, so that applying additional margin is unnecessary to make the distance to the left and the right word equal.\r\n\r\nFurther I think that a tag for general spacing is needed because it is very common. Maybe a bit less in English. It's just as important as bold or italic text and part of the content, so CSS should not be a requirement. For the name I would go with `<sp>` as it's short and resembles the words \"spacing\" in English and „Sperrsatz“ in German making it easy to remember.\r\nIt would also be easily implementable in all browsers.\r\nIn Chrome I think it would be as easy as adding `sp {letter-spacing:0.125em;}` to the default style sheet. I recommend this value as it has been a standard in Germany and is not too intrusive.",
